ailures, cycle validation before scheduling, deterministic wave ordering, and transitive blocking?\"\n  }\n}\n\n## role\n\nImplement the selected task and demonstrate it; leave changes uncommitted.","time":{"created":1789215269202}},{"id":"msg_ZGVsaXZlcnkuMS90YXNrLjEvd29ya2VyLjE_00000000000000000004","type":"assistant","agent":"worker","model":{"id":"gpt-5.6-luna","providerID":"openai"},"content":[{"type":"reasoning","text":"","time":{"created":1789215273283,"completed":1789215275861}},{"type":"text","text":"I’m using the repository protocol and proof-of-work guidance because this is a scoped implementation with an executable contract. I’ll inspect the current API/spec and fixture, implement the CLI and focused tests, then run the tests and external acceptance without touching its script."}],"time":{"created":1789215271104,"streamed":1789215280467,"completed":1789215280467},"finish":"unknown","cost":0,"tokens":{"cache":{"read":9984,"write":0},"input":12416,"output":248,"reasoning":140}},{"id":"msg_ZGVsaXZlcnkuMS90YXNrLjEvd29ya2VyLjE_00000000000000000062","type":"assistant","agent":"worker","model":{"id":"gpt-5.6-luna","providerID":"openai"},"content":[{"type":"tool","id":"exec-36b108d5-1a6f-4b69-91cf-b8110cf29942","name":"commandExecution","time":{"created":1789215280540,"ran":1789215280540,"completed":1789215280541},"state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /Users/tyler/.agents/skills/agent-protocol/SKILL.md \u0026\u0026 printf '\\\\n--- proof-of-work ---\\\\n' \u0026\u0026 sed -n '1,260p' /Users/tyler/.agents/skills/proof-of-work/SKILL.md \u0026\u0026 printf '\\\\n--- memory hits ---\\\\n' \u0026\u0026 rg -n -i 'merge.queue|loop-api|dependency|wave|acceptance|SPEC.md' /Users/tyler/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md | head -80\"","cwd":"/Users/tyler/.codex/worktrees/loop-api-review/gimble/ephemeral/review/loop-api/workspace"},"content":[{"text":"---\nname: agent-protocol\ndescription: \u003e\n  Read at the start of every session and after context compaction to understand\n  organization requirements and expected agent behavior.\n---\n\n1. Do task work in a worktree. Use the root checkout only for orientation and\n   fast-forward synchronization.\n2. For non-trivial tasks, maintain a worklog using `/session-worklog`. Record\n   actionable intelligence, not routine activity.\n3. Keep temporary operating material under `ephemeral/`. Never write to `docs/`\n   without express permission.\n4. Append to the worklog and make a checkpoint commit after each task. Do not\n   rerun checks solely because of the commit. Complete the relevant proof and\n   correctness protocol before declaring the PR merge-ready.\n5. Squash-merge PRs. Write the PR body so it can serve as the squash commit\n   message; put extended write-ups and review discussion in PR comments.\n6. After merge, delete the task worktree and stop its background processes.\n7. Fix broken tests or CI regardless of origin. If a pre-existing failure would\n   materially distract from the task, ask HITL whether to proceed despite it.\n8. Fast-forward the root checkout before starting work and after the PR merges.\n\n`ephemeral/` is tracked working material such as worklogs, plans, and downloaded\nartifacts. Create `ephemeral/projects/\u003cproject-slug\u003e/` only when the user starts\nor names a project, and follow any project list documented by the target repo.\n\n--- proof-of-work ---\n---\nname: proof-of-work\ndescription: \u003e\n  Define and assess proof for application changes. Use while authoring coding\n  tasks, reviewing a PR, or reassessing whether changed behavior was actually\n  demonstrated. Do not use for docs-only changes.\n---\n\n# Proof Of Work\n\nProve changed behavior through the running application and produce an artifact\nthat demonstrates the result. If the repo provides `/repo-proof`, load it for\nthe project-specific protocol.\n\nUnit tests, linters, and compile checks are required checks, not proof, unless\nthey exercise the whole application behavior being claimed.\n\nFor docs, policy, scripts, or tooling, use focused checks instead of full-stack\napplication proof unless the change affects runtime behavior.\n\n## Proof Claims\n\nIn a task or issue, describe the observable claims the implementation must\ndemonstrate. Do not provide a checklist of commands or list routine checks such\nas unit tests and compilation.\n\nIn nlspec-governed work, claims arrive classified *cell-local* (demonstrated\nthrough one cell's contract) or *integration* (demonstrated end-to-end); see\nthe spec-writing standard. Integration claims are demonstrated only through\nthe running application; a cell-local claim may be demonstrated against a\nmock authenticated per slice-design.\n\n## PR Evidence\n\nLead with each claim and what demonstrated it. Put complete, concrete evidence\nin the PR body and upload artifacts instead of citing local paths. Use the\nrepo's proof-artifact uploader when one exists and verify its links.\n\nIf evidence cannot be uploaded, identify what remains local and why. Do not\nclaim complete proof for evidence reviewers cannot inspect.\n\nAt closeout, identify the proved head SHA, satisfied and unmet claims, artifact\nlinks, relevant checks, and any proof blocker.\n\n--- memory hits ---\n147:scope: Temporary workspaces where acceptance must be independently exercised; preserve explicitly staged defects and mark absent execution evidence as unverified.\n150:## Task 1: independently validate the dependency-free Go units CLI Sprint 1, outcome success\n157:- rollout_summaries/2026-09-10T15-44-01-9pmP-complete_units_cli_sprint_1_acceptance.md (cwd=/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-tyler-src-gimble--claude-worktrees-clever-rosalind-44c28b/e8c4bfd6-d77f-4144-b57e-c598ba5e4ceb/scratchpad/live/app3, rollout_path=/Users/tyler/.codex/archived_sessions/rollout-2026-09-10T09-44-01-01a08bfd-7f1c-7200-b7b1-81c28deeb9b3.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-10T15:47:02+00:00, thread_id=01a08bfd-7f1c-7200-b7b1-81c28d