very specified invalid condition with exit 2, useful stderr, and no stdout, and emits deterministic dependency waves plus transitively blocked IDs for valid input. Add focused tests for validation, cycle detection including nonpassing cycles, stable ordering, empty queues, and blocking propagation. The external acceptance program builds the repository root as a binary named mergequeue, so the executable behavior and exit status are the contract; the existing main.go only prints an incomplete empty result and there are no source tests to preserve.\n    definition_of_done: The repository contains a standard-library-only implementation and focused tests that demonstrate the required valid scheduling and invalid-input behavior, including strict rejection of trailing JSON and cycles that contain failed or pending PRs. Valid output uses non-null empty arrays, ascending IDs, and is independent of input order; invalid runs produce no stdout and exit 2 with a nonempty diagnostic. The implementation is ready for the separate acceptance/examples task without changing SPEC.md or the external acceptance program.\n    validation:\n      command: go test ./...\n      query: Does the implementation's executable behavior satisfy the full SPEC.md contract, especially exact single-value JSON parsing, exit-2/no-stdout failures, cycle validation before scheduling, deterministic wave ordering, and transitive blocking?\n  - name: Add runnable README examples and external acceptance proof\n    description: Add the short README required by SPEC.md with runnable valid input/output examples, then use the unchanged external acceptance program to demonstrate the built CLI across its valid, invalid, ordering, and randomized cases. Keep any residual defect visible rather than weakening the contract.\n    definition_of_done: README examples accurately describe and reproduce the implemented CLI, and the recorded external acceptance run passes all cases with the required output and failure semantics